    <div class="case" id="example">
      <div class="case-inner">
        <p class="section-kicker">A worked example</p>

        <h2 class="section-heading">
          Confidence is not the same as evidence
        </h2>

        <p class="section-intro">
          A software business doing $248k a month is considering an 8% price
          rise. The comparison favours raising. But look at what that clarity
          is built on.
        </p>

        <div class="case-grid">
          <div>
            <p class="case-quote">
              Raising prices is worth
              <span class="hl">$231,759</span>
              over two years — unless you lose more than
              <span class="hl">9.5% of customers</span>.
              Nobody has measured that.
            </p>

            <p class="case-note">
              That single sentence is the output. Not the 93%, not the expected
              value — the fact that the result is highly dependent on one
              number the business has never tested, and that the line is only
              four percentage points away from where they guessed.
            </p>

            <p class="case-note">
              It changes what you do next. Rather than committing on an
              untested assumption, you now know exactly what to measure —
              actual volume loss — and how much precision you need before the
              comparison changes.
            </p>

            <p class="case-note">
              A spreadsheet can model uncertainty. BARS is built around the
              decision: compare the options, identify the assumption carrying
              the result, find where the comparison flips, and show you what is
              worth testing next.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="case-panel">
            <h3>What the run actually returned</h3>

            <div class="metric-row">
              <span class="metric-label">Better outcome in</span>
              <span class="metric-value">93% of scenarios</span>
