    <section class="block" id="how-it-works">
      <p class="section-kicker">How it works</p>

      <h2 class="section-heading">
        From a conversation to a defensible comparison
      </h2>

      <p class="section-intro">
        No distributions to choose, no correlation matrices to configure. You
        give ranges instead of false precision; the engine does the rest and
        shows its working.
      </p>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step-card">
          <p class="step-num">01</p>
          <h3 class="step-title">Frame the decision</h3>
          <p class="step-desc">
            Hire now or wait. Raise prices or hold. In-house or outsource.
            Five two-option decisions, each a real question rather than a
            calculator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card">
          <p class="step-num">02</p>
          <h3 class="step-title">Describe what you don't know</h3>
          <p class="step-desc">
            “Revenue is probably $235k–$262k, most likely $248k.” If a figure
            is a guess, say so — it gets flagged rather than treated as fact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card">
          <p class="step-num">03</p>
          <h3 class="step-title">Compare 100,000 scenarios</h3>
          <p class="step-desc">
            Both options face identical simulated conditions, so the comparison
            is like-for-like rather than two separate forecasts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card">
          <p class="step-num">04</p>
          <h3 class="step-title">Find what could change the result</h3>
          <p class="step-desc">
            The engine re-runs across the plausible range of every assumption
            to find which one the result rests on, and where the comparison
            flips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="step-card">
          <p class="step-num">05</p>
          <h3 class="step-title">Know what to test next</h3>
          <p class="step-desc">
            The flip point points to what's worth measuring, piloting, or
            negotiating before you commit the money — not just that you should
            be careful.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="principle">
      <div class="principle-inner">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Why trust the number</p>

          <h2 class="section-heading">
            The model calculates. Nothing is assumed quietly.
          </h2>

          <p class="section-intro" style="margin-bottom: 0">
            AI can help structure the decision and its assumptions, but the
            calculation engine operates on the resulting model, not on a
            conversational prompt — it accepts a structured model and nothing
            else, and cannot be talked into an answer. Every default it applies
            is reported, not buried, and where an input would decide the result
            by its absence, the engine refuses to run rather than filling it
            in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="chain-row">
            <span class="chain-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <div>
              <div class="chain-title">
                279 automated tests, two independent adversarial reviews
              </div>

              <div class="chain-desc">
                Both reviews were adversarial, and both found real defects.
                Every finding is fixed or documented with a reason.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="chain-row">
            <span class="chain-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>

            <div>
              <div class="chain-title">
                Reproducible from the model, seed and engine version
              </div>

