    <section class="block" id="faq">
      <p class="section-kicker">Questions</p>

      <h2 class="section-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-list">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            What does BARS actually tell me?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              Which of two options produces the better outcome across thousands
              of simulated scenarios, how much better, how much room for error
              there is, and — most usefully — which single assumption the result
              is resting on and the exact value at which the comparison flips.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            How is this different from Excel or @RISK?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              Excel and specialist risk tools like @RISK give you powerful
              building blocks for modelling uncertainty. BARS packages those
              capabilities around a specific decision workflow: compare two
              options, identify the assumption carrying the result, and find
              the boundary at which the comparison changes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            Do I need a statistics background?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              No. You give a likely value and a low and a high — the way you
              would describe it to a colleague. The engine handles the
              distribution. Every technical choice it makes on your behalf is
              stated in the report rather than applied silently, so you can
              check it or hand it to someone who will.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            How do I know the numbers are right?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              Every run carries a random seed and an engine version, so the same
              model reproduces the same figures exactly. Every report includes
              a technical appendix with the equations, the provenance of every
              assumption, and every default the engine applied. The engine has
              279 automated tests and has been through two independent
              adversarial reviews.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            What if an assumption is just a guess?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              Say so, and it is recorded as an estimate rather than treated as
              fact. It appears in the provenance trail, in the
              flagged-assumptions list, and as the assumption the decision may
              turn on. Which is exactly what you want to discover before
              committing, rather than after.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            What decisions can it model?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
            <p>
              Five two-option decisions: hire now or wait, in-house or outside
              provider, invest or don't, raise prices or hold, and proceed or
              hold on a plan. If your decision does not fit one of those, the
              software tells you so rather than approximating it with a model
              that does not match.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-q" aria-expanded="false">
            Can you just run one for me?
            <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>

          <div class="faq-a">
